<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bruges</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Brugge</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bruges is a city in West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, linked to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> by canals. From the 12th to the 15th century it was the commercial capital of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, home of a Hanseatic office and Italian banking houses, a leading centre of the Flemish cloth trade and of painters such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-van-eyck">Jan van Eyck</a>. The silting of its waterway shifted trade to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>, leaving a well-preserved medieval centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bruges.jpg" alt="Bruges" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zairon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Br%C3%BCgge_Blick_vom_Belfried_4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the couple sought the protection of the pope, Charles was reconciled with them and gave Baldwin lands around <b>Bruges</b> in the coastal district called Flanders, which he was to defend against Viking raids.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-flanders">County of Flanders · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Catalan, Valencian and Majorcan merchants traded cloth, grain, salt, coral, saffron and slaves across the Mediterranean, held consulates from <b>Bruges</b> to Alexandria and followed the maritime customs of the Consolat de Mar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The first general diet, the Hansetag, met in Lübeck in 1356 to deal with problems at the <b>Bruges</b> office, and historians usually date the “Hansa of the towns” from then; the expression stede van der dudeschen hanse appears in documents soon afterwards.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Order held a monopoly on Baltic amber, selling much of the raw amber to rosary makers in Lübeck and <b>Bruges</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Skilled Protestant craftsmen from Antwerp, <b>Bruges</b> and Ghent moved north after Antwerp fell to Spain in 1585.</blockquote><a href="/societies/dutch-republic">Social Hierarchy of the Dutch Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>At his wedding celebrations in <b>Bruges</b>, the Duke of Burgundy created an exclusive order of knighthood.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-10">10 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
